===== APEX_GEN5.md:901-984 (baseline) =====
   901	### 2.3 Snapshot / Point-in-Time (PIT) Window Management
   902
   903	**Mission:** define `as_of` — the single, dynamic point-in-time boundary a
   904	decision is made against — plus per-consumer observation windows, multi-
   905	timeframe (MTF) alignment, and multi-symbol/multi-timeframe scope, so that no
   906	computation can ever see data from the future.
   907
   908	### `as_of` definition
   909
   910	`as_of` is **not** a fixed 5-second tick — it is the point-in-time boundary
   911	derived from the most recent fully-closed data that satisfies every
   912	consumer's stated requirements:
   913
   914	```
   915	as_of = max(availability_time of all required artifacts)
   916	        where required_timeframes[], minimum_bars, closed_only=true,
   917	        freshness_reference <= threshold(tf)
   918	```
   919
   920	Each consumer declares its own `required_timeframes[]`, `minimum_bars`,
   921	`closed_only`, and `freshness_reference`. A Snapshot is only built once every
   922	required artifact's `availability_time ≤ as_of` (the PIT rule). Example: a
   923	1-hour BOS detector needs 20 closed bars; the last closed 1h candle is at
   924	14:00 with `availability_time` 14:00:03, so `as_of = 14:00:03`. If any
   925	required artifact's availability is 14:00:10 (after `as_of`), the Snapshot is
   926	not built until that artifact arrives, or the consumer downgrades/refuses.
   927
   928	This PIT rule is what prevents: future candles, future OI, future
   929	corrections, future parameter packages/models/calibration/labels, future
   930	portfolio state, centered lookahead windows, and unfinished higher-timeframe
   931	bars from leaking into any calculation.
   932
   933	### Observation window per consumer
   934
   935	`observation_window = {start = as_of − bars×tf_duration, end = as_of,
   936	timeframe, bars = required_depth_bars + warmup_bars}`, built by the Data
   937	Manager from each consumer's dependency graph — **not** a fixed count. For
   938	example: a Structure engine's BOS detector needs 20 bars on 1h; a Volatility
   939	engine's `ATR_14` needs 14 bars plus 14 bars warmup on 1h; a Temporal Context engine
   940	might need 100 bars on 1h. Each gets its own window sized to its own
   941	declared need, all bound to the same `as_of`.
   942
   943	### MTF (multi-timeframe) alignment
   944
   945	All decision-relevant timeframe states are read from one frozen
   946	`SnapshotBarrier` with a single `as_of` boundary. A timeframe may be
   947	absent/insufficient without invalidating unrelated timeframes, but any
   948	consumer whose required timeframe cannot satisfy its freshness/depth
   949	contract must downgrade or refuse the dependent calculation, rather than
   950	silently proceeding. States: `ALIGNED`, `PARTIALLY_ALIGNED`, `CONFLICTING`,
   951	`INSUFFICIENT`. Example: a 1h BOS setup needs 1m, 1h, and 4h context; at
   952	`as_of` 14:00:03, 1m has 100 bars available (OK), 1h has 20 bars (OK), but 4h
   953	only has 10 of the 20 bars it needs — the 4h state is `INSUFFICIENT`, and the
   954	consumer must downgrade or refuse rather than reporting a false `ALIGNED`.
   955
   956	Each timeframe within a Snapshot separately tracks its own `computed_at`,
   957	`source_snapshot_id`, `lookback_policy`, `regime_scope`, and
   958	`expires_at`/freshness — using one Snapshot-wide `as_of` prevents a subtle PIT
   959	violation where one timeframe's future data could otherwise combine with
   960	another timeframe's past data.
   961
   962	### Multi-symbol / multi-timeframe scope
   963
   964	- `symbol_scope`: a list of up to 10 symbols (e.g. for a BTC/ETH correlation
   965	  calculation, both symbols must be present in one Snapshot sharing one
   966	  `as_of`).
   967	- `timeframe_scope`: a list of up to 14 timeframes.
   968	- An observation/data manifest hash (SHA-256 over canonical JSON, sorted
   969	  keys, no whitespace) covers the full scope.
   970
   971	### Snapshot binding
   972
   973	A Snapshot binds: `snapshot_id` (deterministic SHA-256 identity), `as_of`, `symbol_scope`,
   974	`timeframe_scope`, `source_state`, manifest hash, `parameter_package_id`,
   975	`code_version`, a combined quality state (minimum-veto plus weighted
   976	average), and `created_at`.
   977
   978	**Session-CP-14 (2026-09-17):** D28's PAPER bootstrap binds `parameter_package_id = "cp14_paper_bootstrap-v1.0.0-" + SHA256(canonical_parameter_bytes)[:12]`. Canonical parameter bytes are UTF-8 `canonical_json({filename: parsed_YAML_document, ...})` using the repository's sole canonical serializer over every present governed `params/*.yaml` document; the optional `params/e11_classifier_v1.yaml` is included once. Filenames bind package membership, ordering/formatting/comments do not change parameter values, and any governed-value change yields a new package identity. The full parameter digest remains available for provenance; the snapshot identity itself remains the full 64-hex canonical hash, not the 12-hex package suffix. LIVE obtains no PAPER bootstrap package.
   979
   980	Snapshots are immutable; any new artifact triggers supertemporal_window:
   981	OLD → CORRECTION EVENT → NEW VERSION → SUPERSEDES, producing a new `as_of`
   982	(e.g. 14:00:03 → 14:05:10 when a later artifact arrives), invalidating
   983	dependent evidence, and creating a new Snapshot.
   984

===== APEX_GEN5.md:18360-18393 (baseline) =====
 18360	**Evidence Event — 24-field contract mapped to storage.** The frozen
 18361	24-field contract (Chapter 6 §8) is the semantic reference; no DDL column of
 18362	the Data Plane is removed — columns beyond the contract are implementation
 18363	extensions governed by SL-12.
 18364
 18365	| # | Field (24-field contract) | DDL column | Note |
 18366	|---|---|---|---|
 18367	| 1 | `evidence_id` | `evidence_id` (UUIDv7) | unique evidence key |
 18368	| 2 | `engine_id` | `engine_id` (E01..E12) | producer |
 18369	| 3 | `analyst_version` | (side-table) | SemVer + git40 for reproducibility |
 18370	| 4 | `symbol` | `symbol` | |
 18371	| 5 | `timeframe` | `timeframe` | |
 18372	| 6 | `snapshot_id` | `snapshot_id` | PIT binding |
 18373	| 7 | `event_time` | `timestamp_utc` | event instant (UTC) |
 18374	| 8 | `availability_time` | (from snapshot) | PIT condition |
 18375	| 9 | `observation_window` | (from snapshot) | observation window |
 18376	| 10 | `feature_snapshot_id` | `snapshot_id` | data capture version |
 18377	| 11 | `feature_dependencies` | `parent_ids` | input dependencies |
 18378	| 12 | `condition_state` | `event_type` + `validity` | preconditions |
 18379	| 13 | `direction` | (from `event_type`) | direction |
 18380	| 14 | `strength` | `strength` | intensity on the engine's own scale |
 18381	| 15 | `confidence` | `confidence` | calibrated statistical confidence |
 18382	| 16 | `quality` | `quality` | data quality (zero removes, never permits) |
 18383	| 17 | `validity` | `validity` | conditional contract validity |
 18384	| 18 | `fate_state` | (side-table) | CANDIDATE→CONFIRMED→ACTIVE→{...} |
 18385	| 19 | `age` | (derivable) | evidence age |
 18386	| 20 | `decay` | (derivable, λ = 0.1) | weight decay |
 18387	| 21 | `explanation` | `raw` | human-readable explanation |
 18388	| 22 | `parameter_version` | (side-table) | parameter class + version |
 18389	| 23 | `lineage` | `lineage` | derivation chain down to `observation_id` |
 18390	| 24 | `resolution_class` | (side-table, Q0..QX) | cross-engine reference frame |
 18391
 18392	**Session-CP-14 D31:** The validator is aligned to the documented Q0..QX set, with no schema change.
 18393

